              <p style="font-size:14px;line-height:1.6;color:rgba(245,230,216,0.9);margin:0;">This is when it <em>really</em> turned. The flushes that had been a dozen a day dropped to two or three — some days, none. I slept through the night, night after night, and woke up cool instead of soaked. The fog lifted, my patience came back, my mood leveled out. I stopped scanning every room for the nearest exit. I laughed at something my husband said and realized I hadn't done that in a year. Twenty-five years of telling women to ride it out — and I finally understood how wrong that was. That was the month I cried into my coffee, from pure relief, and started telling every patient.<span style="color:#E2A24C;">*</span></p>
